{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a2cb29595a5cabe4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\to a threshold of some form\n2\tis required regardless; we chose a ranking-based threshold over a\n3\tfixed-probability threshold (e.g., [MATH] ) because the latter is\n4\tsensitive to the absolute calibration of an uncalibrated ensemble\n5\t(§ 4.5 , C4), whereas the former depends\n6\tonly on the relative order of predictions. We acknowledge this is\n7\ta transductive choice and report it as a property of the\n8\tsubmission format rather than as a classifier-internal capability. \n9\t\n10\t 4.7 Official Evaluation Results \n11\t\n12\t The official evaluation, conducted by the ImageCLEF 2026 Deepfake\n13\tTask organizers  [ ImageCLEFDeepfakeTaskOverview2026 ] , was\n14\treleased on 2026-05-15. Table  5 reports\n15\tthe four scored categories and their weighted contributions to the\n16\tFinal Score. \n17\t\n18\t Table 5: Audio Detection official evaluation results. The Final\n19\tScore is the weighted sum of the four category contributions. \n20\t\n21\t Category \n22\t Score \n23\t Weight \n24\t Contribution \n25\t\n26\t Baseline Deepfakes \n27\t [MATH] \n28\t [MATH] \n29\t [MATH] \n30\t\n31\t Organizers Real Data \n32\t [MATH] \n33\t [MATH] \n34\t [MATH] \n35\t\n36\t Organizers Real GT Data \n37\t [MATH] \n38\t [MATH] \n39\t [MATH] \n40\t\n41\t Participant Deepfake Weighted \n42\t [MATH] \n43\t [MATH] \n44\t [MATH] \n45\t\n46\t Final Score \n47\t 0.9522 \n48\t\n49\t The score of [MATH] on participant-generated deepfakes (weight\n50\t [MATH] ) indicates that the v18d ensemble generalized to generator\n51\tfamilies it had not been trained against, which is the central\n52\trobustness claim of this work; cross-generator generalization is\n53\texactly the failure mode that the architectural-insurance design of\n54\t§ 4.6 was intended to address. The\n55\tscore of [MATH] on the organizers’ public real data is consistent\n56\twith the top- [MATH] conservative threshold producing no false\n57\tpositives on that partition. The lower score of [MATH] on the\n58\theld-out organizer ground-truth real data corresponds to an\n59\t [MATH] false-positive rate on a previously unseen real\n60\tdistribution; we defer a mechanistic discussion of this gap, and of\n61\tits possible relationship to the [MATH] non- [MATH]  kHz fraction of\n62\tthe test set, to § 7 . \n63\t\n64\t Note. \n65\t\n66\t All leave-one-speaker-out AUC values reported in this paper are\n67\tcomputed without the § 4.4 sample-rate\n68\trule; that rule was applied only at submission time. This separation\n69\tensures that the reported ceiling values reflect genuine model\n70\tbehavior rather than the trivial detection of resampled fakes.\n71\tTable  6 summarizes, category by category,\n72\twhich quantities are affected by the rule and which cannot be\n73\trecomputed without organizer ground-truth files. \n74\t\n75\t Table 6: Effect of the sample-rate rule\n76\t(§ 4.4 ) on the official Detection categories.\n77\tThe rule was applied at submission time; the without-rule\n78\tcounterfactuals require organizer ground-truth files and cannot be\n79\trecomputed from the public release. \n80\t\n81\t Category (weight) \n82\t With rule (official) \n83\t Without rule \n84\t\n85\t Baseline deepfakes ( [MATH] ) \n86\t\n87\t [MATH] \n88\t — (GT-dependent) \n89\t\n90\t Organizer real ( [MATH] ) \n91\t\n92\t [MATH] \n93\t — (GT-dependent) \n94\t\n95\t Organizer real GT ( [MATH] ) \n96\t\n97\t [MATH] \n98\t — (GT-dependent) \n99\t\n100\t Participant fake, weighted ( [MATH] ) \n101\t\n102\t [MATH] \n103\t — (GT-dependent) \n104\t\n105\t Final Score \n106\t 0.9522 \n107\t — (GT-dependent) \n108\t\n109\t The without-rule counterfactuals require the organizer ground-truth\n110\tpartition, which is not publicly released, so the right column is left\n111\tunspecified. The rule-free measurements that we can compute are\n112\tthe internal leave-one-speaker-out AUCs on our own fake variants,\n113\treported throughout § 4.6 and\n114\t§ 6 , where the sample-rate rule is deliberately not\n115\tapplied. \n116\t\n117\t 5 Cross-Track Insights \n118\t\n119\t 5.1 Cross-Track Asymmetry \n120\t\n121\t We submitted to both the Detection and Generation sub-tasks under\n122\tone single-researcher compute budget (a single A100 GPU), a setting\n123\tmost participating teams did not adopt: a team that enters only one\n124\ttrack cannot measure the other side of the same system. The analysis\n125\tthat follows therefore builds on the generation system described in\n126\t§ 3 and connects its offensive design choices to\n127\tthe detection-side observations reported below. On the\n128\tdefensive side, our Detection submission reached a Final Score of\n129\t [MATH] and identified all ( [MATH] ) participant-generated\n130\tdeepfakes. On the offensive side, our official Generation submission\n131\treached a Final Score of [MATH] , ranking first in the Audio\n132\tGeneration sub-task and evading both detector pools at comparable\n133\trates (participant-built [MATH] , organizer-built [MATH] );\n134\t§ 7.3 discusses why the official entry’s\n135\tcomparable rates diverge from the exploratory file-picked variant\n136\t( [MATH] / [MATH] ). Read\n137\ttogether, these results operationalize the multi-view detection\n138\tprinciple in the representational geometry of an SSL backbone\n139\tensemble. Singh et al.  [ singh2026advancedtts ] establish that\n140\tcomplementary analysis levels (semantic, structural, and signal)\n141\timprove robustness against advanced text-to-speech families. We\n142\tspecify a measurable form of that principle within a six-encoder\n143\tensemble and find that, under our controlled single-team conditions,\n144\tthe relationship between defenders and generators is not\n145\tsymmetric. The defender succeeds when any one of six backbone\n146\tencoders captures an artefact—regions [MATH] (WavLM), [MATH] (the\n147\tcontent-acoustic cluster), and [MATH] (ECAPA-TDNN) each impose an\n148\tindependent constraint—whereas the attacker must simultaneously\n149\tevade every encoder that any opposing detector might deploy. The\n150\tmeasured [MATH] implies that evading\n151\tevery region at once is substantially harder than the marginal\n152\tevasion rate against any single detector would suggest\n153\t(Figure  2 ). The\n154\t [MATH] -versus- [MATH] gap is therefore not a comment on the\n155\trelative quality of our two systems — our official Generation entry\n156\ttraded a modest quality cost (WER [MATH] , CER [MATH] ;\n157\t§ 3.5 ) for the strongest evasion in the\n158\tsub-task, ranking first with a Final Score of [MATH] — but an\n159\tempirical estimate of the asymmetry\n160\tintrinsic to multi-backbone defense within the acoustic-only\n161\trepresentational space we sampled\n162\t(§ 8 discusses extensions to audio-LLM views) in\n163\tthe current text-to-speech landscape; the structural mechanism is\n164\tdeveloped in § 4.6 (the\n165\tarchitectural-insurance frame) and quantified in\n166\t§ 6.2 (the three-region representational\n167\tgeometry). We emphasize that this estimate is bounded by an [MATH] \n168\tteam setting; multi-team replication is the natural next step for\n169\tthe architectural-insurance frame to be tested against\n170\tindependently-built attacker populations. \n171\t\n172\t This cross-track view is reinforced by the Phase 3 complementarity\n173\tanalysis of § 6.1 : under the v8 distribution\n174\tshift the per-fold Pearson correlation between WavLM and XLS-R drops\n175\tfrom [MATH] to [MATH] , with the response concentrated in the same\n176\thardest-hybrid regime that drives the cross-track asymmetry. The\n177\tdefender’s disagreement channel and the attacker’s evasion gap are\n178\ttherefore not independent phenomena but two facets of the same\n179\tsix-encoder representational geometry. The same mechanism that makes\n180\tarchitectural insurance work for Detection\n181\t(§ 4.6 ) also constrains the Generation\n182\tside’s evasion budget — the attacker cannot move all six encoders\n183\tsimultaneously without sacrificing the vocoder-fingerprint coherence\n184\tthat audio quality requires (Insight D, § 5.2 ). \n185\t\n186\t Defender side [MATH] WavLM [MATH] content- acoustic [MATH] ECAPA- TDNN trip? trip? trip? OR-gate Block if ANY trip Attacker side [MATH] WavLM [MATH] content- acoustic [MATH] ECAPA- TDNN pass? pass? pass? AND-gate Evade only if ALL pass Measured: [MATH] [MATH] joint evasion substantially harder than marginal evasion \n187\t\n188\t Figure 2: Structural asymmetry between defender and attacker in a\n189\tmulti-backbone SSL ensemble. The defender succeeds when any single\n190\tregion ( [MATH] WavLM, [MATH] content-acoustic, [MATH] ECAPA-TDNN) detects\n191\tan artefact (OR-gate), whereas the attacker must simultaneously evade\n192\tevery region (AND-gate). The measured inter-region correlation\n193\t [MATH] \n194\t(§ 5.1 ) implies that joint evasion is\n195\tsubstantially harder than marginal evasion against any single\n196\tdetector. \n197\t\n198\t 5.2 Five Generation-to-Detection Insights \n199\t\n200\t During the Generation work we built six internal detectors and\n201\tobserved five artefact patterns that directly informed the\n202\tDetection-side design. We summarize each below with its empirical\n203\tsignature and its design implication; the operational consequences\n204\tare incorporated into the v18d submission strategy\n205\t(§ 4.6 ). \n206\t\n207\t 5.2.1 Insight A — MFCC delta-zero sign reversal in\n208\tF5-TTS \n209\t\n210\t The zero-th MFCC delta coefficient reverses sign between real and\n211\tF5-TTS audio: real recordings produce a mean delta-zero of\n212\tapproximately [MATH] , whereas F5-TTS produces approximately\n213\t [MATH] — an opposite sign with roughly twice the magnitude. We\n214\tverified this only on F5-TTS in the present work; whether it\n215\tgeneralizes to other vocoder families is not yet established, so we\n216\treport it as a preliminary observation rather than a universal\n217\tartefact. The very brittleness of this single-vocoder classical\n218\tartefact is informative in its own right: it motivates our shift\n219\taway from hand-crafted features and toward the multi-backbone SSL\n220\tensemble in the final detection design, where representational\n221\tdiversity (§ 6.2 ) provides the robustness\n222\tthat any single classical detector cannot. \n223\t\n224\t 5.2.2 Insight B — vocoder fingerprint hierarchy \n225\t\n226\t Across four vocoder families on the hardest hybrid attack, the\n227\tself-detector AUC stratifies cleanly by vocoder architecture:\n228\tHiFi-GAN (XTTS v2) [MATH] , the internal CosyVoice 3 vocoder\n229\t [MATH] , Vocos (F5-TTS v1) [MATH] , and Vocos2D (GLM-TTS) [MATH] .\n230\tThe ordering “GAN [MATH] iSTFT [MATH] two-dimensional convolution” was\n231\tconsistent across our six self-detectors, with two-dimensional\n232\tconvolutional vocoders the hardest to detect. The design\n233\timplication is that a Detection ensemble trained primarily on\n234\tGAN-based fakes is structurally vulnerable to non-GAN vocoders —\n235\texactly the scenario that motivated multi-backbone diversification\n236\tand the top- [MATH] conservative threshold submission strategy\n237\t(§ 4.6 ). \n238\t\n239\t 5.2.3 Insight C — multi-model hybridization as the\n240\tstrongest evasion \n241\t\n242\t Single-model fakes were detected at a weighted-mean self-detector\n243\tAUC of [MATH] ; distributing speakers across a four-model hybrid\n244\t(GLM-TTS, F5-TTS v1, XTTS v2, CosyVoice 3) dropped this to\n245\t [MATH] — a non-linear evasion gain of [MATH] . The multi-modal\n246\tfake-feature space collapses the decision boundary of single-model\n247\tdetectors. This is the offensive analogue of architectural\n248\tinsurance: encoder diversity for defenders, generator diversity\n249\tfor attackers. \n250\t\n251\t 5.2.4 Insight D — quality–evasion trade-off \n252\t\n253\t Lower word error rate (higher audio quality) correlated\n254\tmonotonically with higher self-detector AUC (easier detection):\n255\tconfigurations near WER [MATH] were detected at AUC [MATH] ,\n256\twhile configurations near WER [MATH] reached AUC [MATH] .\n257\tWe attribute this to sampling temperature — lower temperature\n258\treduces output variance, sharpens the vocoder fingerprint, and\n259\traises detection AUC. A higher-quality opponent therefore leaves\n260\ta cleaner fingerprint, partially offsetting its quality gain from\n261\ta detector’s perspective. \n262\t\n263\t 5.2.5 Insight E — post-processing eliminates evasion \n264\t\n265\t The most counter-intuitive finding is that post-processing helps\n266\tthe detector rather than the attacker: applying a median filter to\n267\ta four-model hybrid fake raised the MFCC self-detector AUC from\n268\t [MATH] to approximately [MATH] on the hardest hybrid\n269\tdistribution. Speed perturbation, resampling, and high-frequency\n270\tnoise injection showed the same direction — each operation we\n271\ttested monotonically increased AUC. This is the empirical\n272\tfoundation of the sample-rate hard rule\n273\t(§ 4.4 ): non- [MATH]  kHz files in the test set\n274\tare, with high probability, the residual of an opposing team’s\n275\tresampling step, and that residual is detectable. The rule was\n276\tapplied only at submission time; the leave-one-speaker-out AUC\n277\tmeasurements of § 6 deliberately exclude it so\n278\tthat the reported ceilings reflect genuine model behavior, as\n279\tnoted in § 4.7 . \n280\t\n281\t 5.3 Knowledge Transfer Loop \n282\t\n283\t The five insights above describe one direction of transfer,\n284\tGeneration to Detection, and two operational mechanisms drove that\n285\tdirection in our pipeline. Insight E supplied the empirical\n286\tevidence for the sample-rate hard rule\n287\t(§ 4.4 ), and Insight B’s vocoder hierarchy\n288\tinformed the four-backbone diversification behind v18d.\n289\tInsight D’s quality–evasion trade-off further reinforced this\n290\tdirection: higher-fidelity attackers are pushed toward lower\n291\tsampling temperatures, which sharpen rather than mask the vocoder\n292\tfingerprint, providing additional justification for the frozen-SSL\n293\tclassifier pipeline whose features are sensitive to exactly this\n294\tclass of deterministic artefact. \n295\t\n296\t The decisive operational influence was the realization that\n297\t we resampled during our own Generation work, which made it\n298\tnatural to suspect that competing teams had not and that\n299\ttheir submissions would therefore carry a detectable resampling\n300\tresidual. This single observation produced the\n301\t§ 4.4 submission heuristic that contributed\n302\tthe [MATH] score on the organizers’ public real data in our final\n303\tDetection submission. \n304\t\n305\t The reverse direction was empirically weaker but conceptually\n306\tsymmetric. Building six internal Detection backbones during the\n307\tGeneration work surfaced the three-region geometry\n308\t(§ 6.2 ) at a smaller scale than the final\n309\tDetection ensemble. We did not have a controlled mechanism to feed\n310\tthis finding back into Generation — the single-team budget and a\n311\tfixed Generation submission schedule left no room for it — so the\n312\toffensive system did not benefit from a closed loop. Closing that\n313\tloop is one of the future-work directions outlined in\n314\t§ 8 . \n315\t\n316\t The aggregate value of operating both tracks under one compute\n317\tbudget is asymmetric: material defensive improvements (the\n318\tsample-rate rule, the four-backbone insight) but no comparable\n319\toffensive gains. The [MATH] -versus- [MATH] gap of\n320\t§ 5.1 therefore reflects the asymmetric\n321\tmaturity of the transfer loop as much as the structural asymmetry\n322\titself. \n323\t\n324\t 6 Further Experiments \n325\t\n326\t 6.1 LOSO 56-Speaker Extension \n327\t\n328\t We extended the v7b evaluation with an external real source,\n329\tLibriSpeech dev-clean   [ panayotov2015librispeech ] ,\n330\tcontributing [MATH] utterances from [MATH] previously unseen\n331\tspeakers. Combined with the organizer’s [MATH] -speaker reference\n332\tpool, this enlarged the real speaker pool from [MATH] to [MATH] and\n333\tbrought the real-to-fake ratio to a near-balanced [MATH] \n334\t( [MATH] versus [MATH] ). Because the fake pool covers only the\n335\t [MATH] internal speaker identities, the held-out folds remain those\n336\t [MATH] internal speakers while the [MATH] LibriSpeech speakers always\n337\tsit in the training pool; v8 is therefore a controlled real-side\n338\tdistribution shift with the fake distribution held fixed, which\n339\tlets us test whether the architectural-insurance frame survives\n340\tsuch a shift rather than simply adding evaluation folds. \n341\t\n342\t Under v8 the mean LOSO AUC was [MATH] , against v7b’s [MATH] ,\n343\tand the four easier single-vocoder attacks were essentially\n344\tunchanged; only the hardest multi-model hybrid attack moved, with\n345\tthe ensemble AUC dropping from [MATH] to [MATH] . This movement\n346\twas asymmetric across backbones: WavLM fell by [MATH] \n347\t( [MATH] ) while XLS-R rose by [MATH] \n348\t( [MATH] ), and the per-fold Pearson correlation\n349\tbetween the two encoders shifted from [MATH] to [MATH] . We\n350\tinterpret this as confirmation rather than regression: the only AUC\n351\tthat responds to the distribution shift is the same one the\n352\tensemble was designed to protect, and the response flows through\n353\tthe disagreement channel between the encoders — an\n354\tarchitectural-insurance prediction expressed at the\n355\tdata-distribution level. \n356\t\n357\t 6.2 Three-Region Backbone Representational Geometry \n358\t\n359\t The six backbones we evaluated — WavLM  [ chen2022wavlm ] ,\n360\tWav2Vec2-XLS-R-300M  [ babu2022xlsr ] ,\n361\tHuBERT  [ hsu2021hubert ] , the Whisper\n362\tencoder  [ radford2023whisp